The fix keeps the same underlying information available, just moves it behind a fetch:

```js
const SYSTEM_PROMPT = 'You are a payments support agent. Use the lookup_endpoint and lookup_policy tools to find specific details — do not guess at API behavior or policy terms.';

const tools = [
  { name: 'lookup_endpoint', description: 'Fetch the reference section for one named API endpoint.', input_schema: { /* ... */ } },
  { name: 'lookup_policy', description: 'Fetch a specific policy document by name (e.g. "refunds", "chargebacks").', input_schema: { /* ... */ } },
];
```

A refund question now costs the small system prompt plus two tool descriptions (a few hundred tokens total) plus one `lookup_policy('refunds')` call that fetches only the relevant paragraph — instead of 40 pages plus a policy document on every single turn, relevant or not.

In code, this is just a deliberate assembly order rather than an implicit one:

```js
const messages = [
  ...history,                              // accumulated context, oldest first
  { role: 'user', content: latestToolResult }, // most decision-relevant, placed last
];

const response = await client.messages.create({
  model: MODEL,
  system: STABLE_SYSTEM_PROMPT,   // stable prefix — first, and cache-friendly
  tools: TOOL_DEFINITIONS,        // stable, needed every step
  messages,                       // history, freshest content last
});
```

Notice the stable-first ordering does double duty: it's both the more attention-favorable position for content that needs to apply on every step (the earlier hook example's problem was a constraint buried in a *long, undifferentiated middle*, not simply "not last") and the arrangement that lets prompt caching actually cache something, since only an unchanged prefix is cacheable. Ordering for attention and ordering for caching point the same direction here, which is why this is worth doing by default rather than as a special case.

In code, that means the compaction prompt has to ask for something structured, not "summarize this":

```js
async function compactHistory(messages) {
  const older = messages.slice(0, -10); // keep the last 10 raw, compact the rest
  const recent = messages.slice(-10);

  const compacted = await client.messages.create({
    model: MODEL,
    max_tokens: 800,
    system: 'Compact this agent transcript. Extract and return ONLY: (1) concrete decisions made and their specific values (file paths, IDs, config values — not vague descriptions), (2) any still-open questions or unresolved items, (3) the current state of the task. Do NOT include a narrative summary, do NOT include resolved exploratory detail, do NOT include raw tool output that already led to a recorded decision.',
    messages: [{ role: 'user', content: JSON.stringify(older) }],
  });

  return [
    { role: 'user', content: `[Compacted earlier context]: ${compacted.content[0].text}` },
    ...recent,
  ];
}
```

The instruction explicitly rules out the failure mode from the hook — a narrative summary — by naming what to extract instead: decisions with their specific values, and open questions. That's the difference between a compaction a later step can act on and one that only sounds like progress was made.

The fix for "should be fine" is a real count, logged on every step rather than estimated once during development:

```js
import { encoding_for_model } from 'tiktoken'; // or the provider's own token-counting endpoint

function logStepContext(step, messages, system, tools) {
  const tokens = estimateTokens({ messages, system, tools });
  console.log(JSON.stringify({
    step,
    tokens,
    breakdown: {
      system: estimateTokens({ system }),
      tools: estimateTokens({ tools }),
      history: estimateTokens({ messages: messages.slice(0, -1) }),
      latest: estimateTokens({ messages: messages.slice(-1) }),
    },
    costUsd: (tokens / 1_000_000) * INPUT_PRICE_PER_M,
  }));
}

// called before every model call, not just when something goes wrong
logStepContext(stepNumber, messages, system, tools);
const response = await client.messages.create({ model: MODEL, messages, system, tools });
```

Many providers, including Anthropic's API, also expose an actual token-counting endpoint (`client.messages.countTokens(...)`) that returns an exact number rather than an estimate — worth using directly rather than approximating with a generic tokenizer library when precision matters, since exact tokenization can differ slightly between models and library estimates.

