contentSections.body:
A more honest chunker respects natural boundaries and keeps related content together:

```js
function chunkDocument(text, targetSize = 500, overlap = 50) {
  const paragraphs = text.split(/\n\s*\n/); // split on paragraph boundaries first
  const chunks = [];
  let current = '';

  for (const para of paragraphs) {
    if ((current + para).length > targetSize && current.length > 0) {
      chunks.push(current.trim());
      // overlap: carry the tail of the previous chunk forward, so a
      // sentence right at the boundary still has its context in both chunks
      current = current.slice(-overlap) + para;
    } else {
      current += (current ? '\n\n' : '') + para;
    }
  }
  if (current.trim()) chunks.push(current.trim());
  return chunks;
}
```

Two things doing real work here: splitting on paragraph boundaries first (so a chunk edge, when it does have to happen, lands between paragraphs rather than mid-sentence), and a small overlap between consecutive chunks, so content genuinely near a boundary has a chance to appear intact in at least one of the two chunks straddling it.

