The minimum fix passes the actual retrieved chunks into the prompt and constrains citation to what's there:

```js
const retrieved = await retrieveRelevantChunks(query, { topK: 5 });

const answer = await client.messages.create({
  model: MODEL,
  max_tokens: 800,
  system: `Answer using only the sources below. Cite each claim with the source's id in brackets, e.g. [doc-14]. Do not cite anything not listed here.`,
  messages: [{
    role: 'user',
    content: `SOURCES:\n${retrieved.map(r => `[${r.id}] ${r.text}`).join('\n\n')}\n\nQUESTION: ${query}`,
  }],
});
```

Every citation the model can now produce references a chunk id that's provably in `retrieved` — there's no path to citing something that was never actually given to it.

In code, this means the citation is a structured object built from retrieval metadata, not free text the model invents:

```js
// each retrieved chunk already carries this from the retrieval layer
// { id: 'doc-14-chunk-3', docTitle: 'Deployment Guide', sectionHeading: '4.2 Rollback', text: '...' }

const citation = {
  docTitle: chunk.docTitle,
  chunkId: chunk.id,           // stable regardless of later edits or re-pagination
  sectionHeading: chunk.sectionHeading,
  quote: extractedSupportingSpan, // the exact text the claim rests on
};
```

Building the citation from the chunk's own stored metadata — rather than letting the model free-write a page reference — is what keeps it locatable even as the underlying document keeps changing.

The fix is explicit, structural permission and a shape for saying so:

```js
const system = `Answer only using the sources below. Cite each claim with its source id.
If the sources do not address part of the question, say so explicitly in "unsupportedAspects" —
do not fill gaps with general knowledge, even if you are confident it's likely correct.
Respond as JSON: { "answer": string, "citations": [...], "unsupportedAspects": string[] }`;

const result = await client.messages.create({
  model: MODEL, max_tokens: 800, system,
  messages: [{ role: 'user', content: `SOURCES:\n${sourcesText}\n\nQUESTION: ${query}` }],
});
```

On the annual-plan question, this shape forces a real choice: an empty `answer` about annual plans specifically, with `unsupportedAspects: ["Annual plan refund terms are not addressed in the retrieved Terms of Service"]` — instead of a fabricated 30-day figure with nothing flagging that it isn't real.

