In practice this is one more field on the same structured output:

```json
{
  "answer": "Escalate after 5 minutes, per the current incident-response doc.",
  "citations": [{ "chunkId": "incident-doc-chunk-2", "quote": "On-call must escalate unresolved P1 incidents after 5 minutes." }],
  "conflictingSources": [{
    "chunkId": "runbook-chunk-9",
    "quote": "Escalate after 15 minutes if unresolved.",
    "note": "Older document (last updated 2 years prior); incident-response doc appears to supersede it, but this wasn't independently confirmed"
  }]
}
```

That last clause matters — the agent stating *why* it preferred one source doesn't mean the preference is certainly correct, and saying so plainly leaves room for a human reader who might know the runbook is still the operative policy for a specific team.

The verification pass, run against every claim-citation pair, not a sample:

```js
async function verifyGrounding(claim, citedChunk) {
  const check = await client.messages.create({
    model: MODEL,
    max_tokens: 200,
    system: `You are checking whether a SOURCE passage actually supports a CLAIM.
Respond as JSON: { "supported": boolean, "reason": string }.
"supported" is true only if the source explicitly states or directly implies the claim —
not merely if the source is on the same general topic.`,
    messages: [{ role: 'user', content: `CLAIM: ${claim}\n\nSOURCE: ${citedChunk.text}` }],
  });
  return JSON.parse(check.content[0].text);
}

async function verifyOutput(claimsWithCitations) {
  const results = await Promise.all(
    claimsWithCitations.map(async (c) => ({
      claim: c.text,
      ...(await verifyGrounding(c.text, c.citedChunk)),
    })),
  );
  return results.filter((r) => !r.supported); // the ones worth flagging for review
}
```

